swift-demangle itself refuses.

================================================================================
Nim demangling -- src/demangle/schemes/nim/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The mangling rules are transcribed from the Nim compiler's own
compiler/mangleutils.nim (identifier escaping) and compiler/modulegraphs.nim
(module path encoding), which are the only description of them that exists --
Nim ships no demangler. Nim is licensed under the MIT License; see
https://github.com/nim-lang/Nim/blob/devel/copying.txt.

================================================================================
Nim conformance corpus -- tests/conformance/nim-real-world.txt
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Produced by compiling purpose-built Nim programs with the shipped Nim 1.6.14 and
Nim 2.2.0 compilers and reading the .ndi debug-mapping files they write. The
mangled names and the source names are the compilers' own output.

================================================================================
Free Pascal demangling -- src/demangle/schemes/pascal/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The mangling rules are transcribed from the Free Pascal compiler's own
compiler/symdef.pas (make_mangledname, mangledprocparanames, rtti_mangledname),
compiler/symsym.pas (variable and typed-constant prefixes), compiler/ncgvmt.pas
(interface tables and wrappers) and compiler/symtable.pas (operator names), which
are the only description of them that exists -- Free Pascal ships no demangler.
The compiler is licensed under the GNU General Public License version 2 or later;
its runtime library is under the LGPL with a static-linking exception. No Free
Pascal code is included here: the Python is an independent implementation of the
rules those files describe.

================================================================================
Free Pascal conformance corpus -- tests/conformance/pascal-real-world.txt
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The mangled names are symbols from the object files of the Free Pascal 3.2.2
runtime and packages as shipped by Debian/Ubuntu. The expected column is this
library's own reading; see tests/test_pascal.py for what establishes it.

================================================================================
Delphi/C++Builder conformance corpora -- tests/conformance/delphi-tdump.txt,
tests/conformance/delphi-real-world.txt
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Both columns are the borland.yaml sample from the unmangler project by Andrey
"Zed" Zaikin (https://github.com/zed-0xff/unmangler), used under the MIT License.
That file is a dump the project's misc/2_tdump.rb produced by running Embarcadero's
own tdump.exe -q -um over the export tables of real Delphi BPLs and C++Builder
DLLs: the mangled names are those exports, and the expected column is what that
unmangler printed for each. Reformatted here as tab-separated rows, and split into
the whole table and a per-kind sample of it.

